Run via Docker Compose:
Build and start the containerized application:  Bashdocker-compose up --build
Access the API:Interactive API Documentation (Swagger UI): http://localhost:8000/docsHealth Check: http://localhost:8000/health  2. Authentication & Authorization (SSO/OIDC Extension)Currently, the system uses basic JWT authentication. To extend this to a production Single Sign-On (SSO) or OpenID Connect (OIDC) architecture:  Flow: The frontend application redirects the user to an Identity Provider (IdP) like Okta or Auth0. Upon successful login, the IdP returns an OAuth2/OIDC compliant JWT.  API Gateway: The API Gateway intercepts requests, validating the JWT's signature using the IdP's public keys before forwarding it to the AI Service.  Role-Based Access Control (RBAC): Claims embedded in the JWT dictate permissions.  Admin: Possesses the admin claim, granting access to /metrics and configuration endpoints.  User: Granted the standard user claim, allowing POST requests to the /chat endpoint.  Read-Only: Granted a restricted claim, allowing access to view historical permitted reports without executing new LLM generations.  3. Scaling Scenario: Handling 100 to 500 RPSIf traffic spikes from 100 to 500 requests per second, the synchronous LLM calls will bottleneck the system. Here is how the architecture adapts:  Horizontal Scaling & Load Balancing: FastAPI instances are scaled horizontally using Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA). An AWS Application Load Balancer (ALB) distributes incoming traffic across the healthy pods.  Redis Caching: Identical questions are served directly from the Redis cache, returning an answer instantly with zero LLM API cost.  Rate Limiting: Redis is utilized as a distributed rate limiter (e.g., Token Bucket algorithm) to throttle aggressive users and protect the underlying LLM provider from being overwhelmed.  Background Queues & Concurrent Requests: Synchronous /chat endpoints are converted to an asynchronous task pattern (e.g., using Celery/RabbitMQ). The API accepts the question, pushes it to a background queue, and returns a task_id. Worker nodes pull from the queue, allowing thousands of concurrent requests to buffer safely without crashing the web servers.  Failure Recovery: If the LLM provider times out or fails, a Circuit Breaker pattern opens to prevent cascading failures. The system employs exponential backoff for retries and graceful degradation (e.g., returning a polite "Service busy" message or a cached fallback response).  4. Production Architecture & Migration Strategy (EC2 to Kubernetes)Moving from a single EC2 server supporting 10 users to a Kubernetes cluster supporting 10,000 users requires decoupling the application components.  Target Architecture DiagramCode snippetgraph TD
    A[Users] --> B[Load Balancer]
    B --> C[FastAPI Pod 1]
    B --> D[FastAPI Pod 2]
    B --> E[FastAPI Pod N]
    
    C & D & E --> F[(Redis Cache & Rate Limiting)]
    C & D & E --> G[[Message Queue / RabbitMQ]]
    C & D & E --> H[(PostgreSQL)]
    
    G --> I[Background Worker 1]
    G --> J[Background Worker 2]
    
    I & J --> K[LLM Gateway / Circuit Breaker]
    K --> L[External LLM APIs]
Migration & Design DecisionsMinimal Downtime Migration: I would implement a Blue/Green deployment strategy. The new Kubernetes infrastructure (Green) is spun up alongside the existing EC2 server (Blue). DNS records (Route 53) are slowly weighted to send 10% of traffic to the new cluster, monitoring for errors before shifting 100% of the traffic and decommissioning the EC2 instance.  Handling LLM Limits (RPM/TPM): An internal LLM Gateway handles routing. If the primary model hits its Tokens-Per-Minute (TPM) limit, the Gateway automatically falls back to a secondary model or region to ensure uptime.  Handling Slow/Failing Requests: By introducing a Message Queue (RabbitMQ), slow LLM responses no longer tie up FastAPI worker threads. If an LLM request fails completely, the background worker retries the job up to 3 times before moving it to a Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) for manual inspection.  Monitoring: Prometheus scrapes the /metrics endpoint, and Grafana visualizes the data. Alerts are configured to notify the DevOps team via Slack/PagerDuty if latency exceeds 5 seconds or if the error rate spikes above 2%.  Secrets Management: The .env file is replaced by Kubernetes Secrets, securely backed by a provider like AWS Secrets Manager or HashiCorp Vault[cite: 1]. Secrets are injected into the pods at runtime, ensuring no API keys are ever stored on disk[cite: 1].